// `addr(of: …)` needs a PLACE — a field, local, or element. A temporary has no address that outlives the
// expression, and `&(f())` is not even valid C. This diagnostic existed but sat in an unreachable second
// `addr` handler (the guard above it was identical and returned first), so it had never fired: the call
// below used to compile, and only the C compiler complained — about generated code.
fn int32 five() { return 5; }
unsafe fn int run() {
    UnsafePtr<int32> p = addr(of: five());   // ERROR: not a place
    return p[0];
}

// The body above is the raw work, so IT carries the marker; `main` may not be `unsafe`
// (it encloses the whole program). Calling an `unsafe fn` from safe code is allowed.
fn int main() { return run(); }
